spec:
  grid: 7-9 rows x 7-9 cols (square)
  colors_input: exactly 2 — orange (7) background, red (2) wall
  colors_output: red (2) walls unchanged; bg regions recolored grey (5) or green (3)
  wall_segments:
    count: 1-3 (4-connected components of red pixels)
    shape: simple path (no branching); each pixel has <=2 wall neighbors
    turns: 1-5 per segment; alternating horizontal/vertical runs (staircase)
    length: 4-13 pixels per segment
    endpoints: both endpoints of every segment lie on the grid border
    isolation: no two segments are 8-adjacent to each other
  background_regions:
    count: n_walls + 1 (always one more region than wall segments), range 2-4
    topology: region adjacency graph is a tree AND a linear chain (path graph)
    each wall segment separates exactly 2 regions
    lower_left_corner: must be a background pixel (not wall)
  transform:
    keep wall pixels; BFS-color regions alternately grey/green
    region containing lower-left corner gets grey (5)
    adjacent regions across a wall get opposite color
    result is bipartite 2-coloring anchored at lower-left = grey
generation_strategy:
  approach: build walls directly, verify region constraints
  steps:
    1. Pick grid size H=W in [7,9].
    2. Pick n_walls in [1,3]. Will create n_walls staircase wall paths
       that partition the grid into n_walls+1 bg regions in a linear chain.
    3. Generate each wall as a staircase path from one grid border to another,
       alternating H and V runs of length 1-7. Start from a random border cell,
       walk inward with random axis-aligned segments, exit at another border.
    4. Place walls one at a time. Each wall must not be 4-adjacent or 8-adjacent
       to any existing wall. Each wall must separate exactly 2 bg regions.
    5. Validate: lower-left cell is bg, n_regions == n_walls+1, region graph
       is a connected linear chain, graph is bipartite. Retry on failure.
    6. Fill grid with orange bg and red walls. Output: apply BFS 2-coloring.
